# Line endings.
#
# The repository stores LF and the working tree gets LF too, on every machine.
#
# Without this, git on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) writes CRLF into the
# working tree while storing LF, and prettier — whose endOfLine default is
# "lf" — then reports every file in the project as badly formatted. Nothing is
# actually wrong at that point, which is what makes it expensive: `pnpm
# format:check` fails on 81 files that no one touched, and the real fix looks
# like `prettier --write .` rewriting the entire repo.
#
# eol=lf rather than leaving it to autocrlf, because there is nothing here that
# needs CRLF. The one thing that would is a .bat file, and this project has none.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
